<p>The US Senate voted to block a war powers resolution that would have required congressional authorization before President Donald Trump could continue or expand US military action against Iran, a conflict that has rapidly widened across the Middle East. The measure died on a procedural vote. Republicans largely stuck together to stop it from advancing, while a small number of lawmakers crossed party lines. Supporters said Congress needs to reassert its constitutional role in decisions to use force and avoid sliding into an open-ended conflict, arguing the administration has offered shifting explanations for the campaign’s objectives. Opponents said President Trump’s actions protect US interests and argued for continuing the operation.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/Zf_baMObc2PJ4wDvGlDn3JMjXaH8c3xRuj-AYt5G6UijTXmg7sHRtYyUBhIIuA7xqQVrGbTDuvllh6OEYsTK5AJdX1FL3pBuWD6D5U2o_0-O6oztgynsgfHOl_kpjAjTUpbZCpVF1E_cGsGnLUBq-QxCWIv3fugy5KZMY97iGmK5drMqLSOEEgSOO7Pscg' alt='The rubble of a police station in Tehran this week after the U.S.-Israeli airstrikes.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Sponsors and aim: Sens. Tim Kaine, Adam Schiff and Chuck Schumer introduced the resolution to require congressional authorization before hostilities against Iran could continue.</li><li>Cross-party names: Sen. Rand Paul was the only Republican to back the resolution, while Democratic Sen. John Fetterman broke with his party to support the strikes.</li><li>House next: House lawmakers are expected to take up a similar war powers measure, which is also expected to fail.</li><li>Wider regional fallout: Euronews reported explosions in Israel after a US submarine sank an Iranian warship, prompting Tehran to threaten military and economic infrastructure across the region.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Politico: The vote amounted to a fresh endorsement of Trump’s five-day campaign, echoing an identical Senate vote last June after US strikes on Iran’s nuclear facilities. (<a href='https://www.politico.com/news/2026/03/04/senate-rejects-war-powers-trump-00813233'>Politico</a>)</li><li>Senate Majority Leader John Thune: Thune said President Trump was “acting in the best interest of the nation,” and urged colleagues to vote against the resolution. (<a href='https://www.allsides.com/news/2026-03-04-0800/federal-state-and-tribal-powers-senate-vote-war-powers-resolution-prevent-trump-continuing-iran-conflict'>AllSides</a>)</li><li>Pentagon: Defense officials said they would “accelerate” US actions and described the conflict as still in its early phase. (<a href='https://www.scmp.com/news/us/diplomacy/article/3345487/us-senate-rejects-bid-curb-trumps-iran-war-powers-pentagon-set-increase-attacks'>South China Morning Post</a>)</li><li>Christian Science Monitor analysis: The outlet noted that Congress has increasingly let presidents take the lead on war decisions, raising questions about checks and balances and what the framers intended. (<a href='https://www.csmonitor.com/USA/Politics/2026/0304/trump-iran-congress-war-powers'>The Christian Science Monitor</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.thehindu.com/news/international/republicans-vote-down-legislation-to-halt-iran-war-in-congress-first-vote-on-conflict/article70705012.ece'>Republicans vote down legislation to halt Iran war in Congress&#x27; first vote on conflict</a> - thehindu.com</li><li><a href='https://www.csmonitor.com/USA/Politics/2026/0304/trump-iran-congress-war-powers'>Senate rejects bill to curb Trump on Iran. That fits a long pattern on war powers.</a> - csmonitor.com</li><li><a href='https://www.scmp.com/news/us/diplomacy/article/3345487/us-senate-rejects-bid-curb-trumps-iran-war-powers-pentagon-set-increase-attacks'>US Senate rejects bid to curb Trump’s Iran war powers as Pentagon set to increase attacks</a> - scmp.com</li><li><a href='https://www.pbs.org/newshour/politics/watch-live-senate-expected-to-vote-on-war-powers-resolution-after-u-s-israeli-strikes-o